===Etymology===
{{PIE root|en|bʰendʰ}}
{{bor|en|fr|bandage|withtext=1}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|/ˈbændɪdʒ/|lang=en}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# A [[strip]] of [[gauze]] or similar [[material]] used to [[protect]] or [[support]] a [[wound]] or [[injury]].
#* '''1883:''' {{w|Robert Louis Stevenson}}, ''{{w|Treasure Island}}''
#*: {{...}}he was deadly pale, and the blood-stained '''bandage''' round his head told that he had recently been wounded, and still more recently dressed.
# A strip of cloth bound round the head and eyes as a [[blindfold]].
#* '''1844:''' {{w|Alexander Dumas}}, {{w|The Count of Monte Cristo}} [http://en.wikisource.org/wiki/The_Count_of_Monte_Cristo/Chapter_75]
#*: {{...}}the president informed him that one of the conditions of his introduction was that he should be eternally ignorant of the place of meeting, and that he would allow his eyes to be bandaged, swearing that he would not endeavor to take off the '''bandage'''.
#* {{quote-book|year=1963|author={{w|Margery Allingham}}|title={{w|The China Governess}}
|chapter=17|url=http://openlibrary.org/works/OL2004261W
|passage=The face which emerged was not reassuring. It was blunt and grey, the nose springing thick and flat from high on the frontal bone of the forehead, whilst his eyes were narrow slits of dark in a tight '''bandage''' of tissue.&nbsp;[…].}}

